/-!
# Quasi-regular sequences and arbitrary base change with flat quotient

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 0CEP, part (1), and Algebra, Tag 061P
(blueprint: lem-pur-quasi-regular-flat-quotient-base-change).

The existing `Ideal.regularSequenceGradedPolynomialMap` is defined for every finite
sequence, without a regularity assumption. It sends variables to their degree-one
classes and constants to degree zero. Bijectivity therefore expresses exactly the
canonical graded quotient-algebra isomorphism in the quasi-regularity criterion.
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w

open scoped TensorProduct

namespace Ideal

/-- Quasi-regularity survives arbitrary scalar extension when the quotient is flat.
Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 0CEP, part (1); Algebra, Tag 061P
(blueprint: lem-pur-quasi-regular-flat-quotient-base-change).

Proof (the eight steps of the node):
1. Put J = (f) and C = A/J. In degree m, the assumed polynomial isomorphism
   identifies G_m = J^m/J^(m+1) with C indexed by the finitely many monomials of
   degree m. Thus G_m is R-flat by `lem-weil-pbc-finite-product-flat-modules`.
   This includes an empty monomial set and its zero module.
2. Put Q_m = A/J^m for m >= 1. The base case Q_1 = C is flat. For the induction,
   the quotient maps give the exact sequence 0 -> G_m -> Q_(m+1) -> Q_m -> 0:
   a representative dies in Q_m precisely when it lies in J^m.
3. Tensor this sequence with any injection X -> Y. By
   `lem-weil-pbc-flat-quotient-tensor-injective` its two rows are short exact.
   A tensor in X tensor Q_(m+1) dying below first dies in X tensor Q_m by
   flatness of Q_m, so lifts to X tensor G_m. Injectivity of the bottom row
   makes this lift die below, and flatness of G_m makes it zero. Thus Q_(m+1)
   is flat, completing the induction. The squares commute on pure tensors.
4. The image of u_m : J^m tensor R' -> A tensor R' is an ideal. For m >= 1,
   expand products of generators: J^m is generated by degree-m monomials in f,
   whose tensor images are those in f' = f tensor 1. Hence im(u_m) = J'^m.
   At m = 0 the map is the identity, with image the whole ring.
5. Tensor 0 -> J^m -> A -> Q_m -> 0. Flatness of Q_m and
   `lem-weil-pbc-flat-quotient-tensor-injective` make u_m injective, so
   J^m tensor R' identifies with J'^m. The m = 1 quotient gives the ring
   isomorphism C tensor R' = C', sending [a] tensor s to [a tensor s].
   Its multiplication and unit formulas follow on pure tensors.
6. By `lem-weil-pbc-tensor-right-exact`, tensoring J^(m+1) -> J^m -> G_m -> 0
   identifies G_m tensor R' with J'^m/J'^(m+1). Under step 5 the first map is
   the actual inclusion. The comparison sends [x] tensor s to [x tensor s];
   it is C'-linear because both scalar-action formulas give [ax tensor ts].
7. Tensor the degree-m isomorphism of step 1 and its inverse. Tensor commutes
   with the finite coordinate product, with inverse given by summing coordinate
   inclusions. Steps 5 and 6 therefore give the degree-m canonical polynomial
   bijection over C', taking a coordinate vector to the corresponding f' monomial.
8. Decompose into finitely many homogeneous parts. Degreewise injectivity gives
   injectivity of the canonical map, and lifting finitely many parts gives
   surjectivity. Its inverse respects algebra operations by injectivity and
   preserves degrees by the direct-sum decomposition, hence it is the required
   graded C'-algebra isomorphism. This includes b = 0 and C' = 0. -/
theorem quasiRegular_flatQuotient_baseChange
    {R : Type u} {A : Type v} {R' : Type w}
    [CommRing R] [CommRing A] [CommRing R'] [Algebra R A] [Algebra R R']
    {b : ℕ} (f : Fin b → A) [Module.Flat R (A ⧸ Ideal.span (Set.range f))]
    (hf : Function.Bijective (regularSequenceGradedPolynomialMap f)) :
    Function.Bijective
      (regularSequenceGradedPolynomialMap (fun j ↦ f j ⊗ₜ[R] (1 : R'))) := by
  sorry

end Ideal
